      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, there is a distinct difference in how the first mention works versus subsequent ones for Chicago, and it depends entirely on which of the two major systems you are using: Notes-Bibliography (NB) or Author-Date (AD).\n\nIn the **Notes-Bibliography** system, the first mention in the text is a full footnote or endnote listing all authors, followed by a superscript number. For your five or six authors, you would write something like \"Smith, Jones, Doe, Brown, Green, and Miller (2023) argue...\" and then put a tiny number at the end. In that footnote, you list all six names again. However, for the *second* mention, you switch to a shortened form using \"et al.\" (meaning \"and others\"), so you would just write \"Smith et al.\" in the text and refer to the original footnote number again.\n\nIn the **Author-Date** system, the logic is slightly different regarding the first mention. If you are using this system, you typically list all authors on the very first citation if there are five or six, just like you do in APA. But for *subsequent* citations, you immediately switch to \"et",
