      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Oh, you are absolutely right to feel like that is a total nightmare, but don't panic, because even the most chaotic lists of thirty authors have a very specific, almost ritualistic way of being handled in both styles.\n\nIn **APA 7th**, if you hit that massive wall of thirty authors, you stop listing names after the first ten. You write the first ten names, followed by an ellipsis (...), and then the last author's name. It's like a long line of marching soldiers where you only name the first ten and the last one before the rest fade into the fog. So it would look like: \"Author 1, Author 2, ..., Author 30.\"\n\nFor **Chicago style**, the rules are actually a bit more generous with the ellipsis. In the **Notes-Bibliography** system, you list the first ten authors, add the ellipsis, and then list the last author. It's the same \"first ten, then last\" rule, but the formatting of the footnote itself can sometimes get a bit messy with that many names crammed together. In the **Author-Date** system, it's identical to APA: you list the first ten, put the dots,",
